WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
Make priorities explicit

2
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:08.300
Carole is the BHW in this fictional classroom exercise.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.600
Three households reportedly missed scheduled visits.

4
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:20.060
This is only a report; the reasons and the number affected across the barangay are unknown.

5
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:26.320
The new dialogue is an authoring adaptation, not a DOH quotation.

6
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:40.940
For planning, score each problem from 1 to 5 on four criteria: magnitude or number affected; severity of the effects; feasibility with available resources; and urgency.

7
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:46.040
Higher feasibility means more achievable, not harder to do.

8
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:51.740
The report from three households is not enough to assign local scores.

9
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.400
Carole: “What supports each score?

10
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.700
Who else should we hear from?”

11
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.360
Add the four scores and discuss evidence with the group.

12
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:06.260
A high total suggests a starting priority.

13
00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:15.000
Refer someone needing urgent care immediately under the appropriate referral procedure; do not wait for a planning score.

14
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.940
Priorities need explicit evidence; they do not replace urgent referral.

15
00:01:21.006 --> 00:01:23.846
Try scoring priorities

16
00:01:24.146 --> 00:01:29.826
These teaching-deck example rows are separate from the report about three households.

17
00:01:30.126 --> 00:01:35.166
They are fictional practice scores, not measurements of your barangay.

18
00:01:35.466 --> 00:01:42.506
The four scores are ordered: magnitude, severity, feasibility, urgency.

19
00:01:42.806 --> 00:01:54.046
BHW training gap: 4 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 19.

20
00:01:54.346 --> 00:02:02.746
Safe-water teaching gap: 4 + 3 + 5 + 5 = 17.

21
00:02:03.046 --> 00:02:12.966
Household water-preparation knowledge gap: 3 + 3 + 5 + 5 = 16.

22
00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:23.866
Carole: “In the first row, 4 + 5 = 9; add 5 = 14; add 5 = 19.”

23
00:02:24.166 --> 00:02:29.446
In the second: 7, 12, 17.

24
00:02:29.746 --> 00:02:34.706
In the third: 6, 11, 16.

25
00:02:35.006 --> 00:02:42.246
Thus 19 ranks first, 17 second and 16 third in this example.

26
00:02:42.546 --> 00:02:47.306
This does not establish such training gaps or risks locally.

27
00:02:47.606 --> 00:02:57.686
Before using a score in a real plan, obtain relevant counts, effects, available resources and reasons for urgency.

28
00:02:57.986 --> 00:03:01.786
Explain the scores before trusting the total.

29
00:03:01.838 --> 00:03:03.998
What supports the score?

30
00:03:04.298 --> 00:03:08.498
Carole: “Three missed visits were reported.

31
00:03:08.798 --> 00:03:12.118
Does that immediately make magnitude a 4?”

32
00:03:12.418 --> 00:03:15.618
Resident partner: “Not yet.

33
00:03:15.918 --> 00:03:21.158
We do not know the overall count or whether the reasons are the same.”

34
00:03:21.458 --> 00:03:27.938
List the report and what remains unknown; do not fill gaps with guesses.

35
00:03:28.238 --> 00:03:37.438
For each score, ask: Magnitude—how many people or households, over what period, and what is the source?

36
00:03:37.738 --> 00:03:42.178
Severity—what effects have supporting evidence?

37
00:03:42.478 --> 00:03:50.438
Feasibility—which people, time, materials and permissions are actually available?

38
00:03:50.738 --> 00:03:54.938
Urgency—why give this planning priority now?

39
00:03:55.238 --> 00:03:59.958
Silence, a nod or an expression does not prove agreement.

40
00:04:00.258 --> 00:04:06.178
Labels such as “lazy” or “uncooperative” are not evidence.

41
00:04:06.478 --> 00:04:12.878
The 1–5 scores are ordered categories, not necessarily equal intervals.

42
00:04:13.178 --> 00:04:25.938
Addition is a simple equal-weight planning aid: all four criteria have equal weight in this example, but it is not a validated formula or clinical triage tool.

43
00:04:26.238 --> 00:04:29.838
Equal totals can hide different effects.

44
00:04:30.138 --> 00:04:38.298
Record the evidence, unknowns and verification questions; discuss these limits before deciding.

45
00:04:38.598 --> 00:04:43.158
Do not turn a guess into a fact to complete the table.

46
00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.180
Resolve a tie; proceed with urgent care

47
00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:54.480
In a separate fictional tie exercise, Plan A and Plan B both score 17.

48
00:04:54.780 --> 00:05:01.180
Plan A clarifies visit information; staff time is available within the role-play.

49
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:07.280
Plan B arranges transport; a vehicle and partner are unconfirmed.

50
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:11.340
No real schedule or funding is promised.

51
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.720
This is not a new scoring of the three deck rows.

52
00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.020
Carole: “The totals match, but which resources are confirmed?”

53
00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:29.720
Partner: “There is time to clarify information for Plan A; transport for B is still a question.”

54
00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.980
Revisit evidence for each score and urgency.

55
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:37.800
Correct an error with its supporting evidence.

56
00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.460
Record unknowns where evidence is missing.

57
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.680
Ask each partner for reasons before agreeing.

58
00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:50.740
Do not choose solely by list order or a leader’s preference.

59
00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.440
Planning and urgent referral are separate.

60
00:05:54.740 --> 00:06:02.700
If someone needs urgent care, proceed with referral under the appropriate procedure while planning discussion continues.

61
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.560
Completing the table is not a prerequisite.

62
00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:11.060
This exercise teaches no new clinical triage rule.

63
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.880
A tie calls for clarification, not invented extra points.

64
00:06:16.947 --> 00:06:20.827
Try, give feedback, then repeat

65
00:06:21.127 --> 00:06:29.007
Use the score/evidence cards, role cards, observer checklist and row-by-row tally aid.

66
00:06:29.307 --> 00:06:35.947
Calculate all three deck totals: 19, 17 and 16.

67
00:06:36.247 --> 00:06:41.487
Explain the ranking and why higher feasibility means more achievable.

68
00:06:41.787 --> 00:06:47.947
Give an evidence question for each criterion before applying scores locally.

69
00:06:48.247 --> 00:06:53.607
On the separate tie card, Plans A and B both score 17.

70
00:06:53.907 --> 00:07:04.107
Discuss available staff time for A and unconfirmed transport for B; ask a partner for reasons and record the agreement and unknowns.

71
00:07:04.407 --> 00:07:10.127
Explain why urgent referral proceeds even if ranking is unfinished.

72
00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:20.107
In a triad, rotate BHW/facilitator, resident-or-partner/recorder and observer.

73
00:07:20.407 --> 00:07:33.367
Each learner gets a 3-minute attempt, 1 minute of specific feedback and a 1-minute retry; three rotations plus 1 minute for transitions total 16 minutes.

74
00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:37.187
Correct an arithmetic or evidence error.

75
00:07:37.487 --> 00:07:43.447
Alone, write the answer with the key covered, then check and retry the error.

76
00:07:43.747 --> 00:07:47.467
Self-review is not direct assessment.

77
00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:51.887
At the next planning meeting, ask where the scores came from.

78
00:07:52.187 --> 00:07:57.827
Feedback identifies a specific correction and includes a fresh attempt.
